course: dictionary
lesson: 3651
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A firework is a small device with powder inside. When it lights, it explodes and makes bright colours, sparks and loud bangs in the sky. The sentence «Watch fireworks from a safe distance and never pick up a used firework; they burn very hot» tells you that fireworks are hot and dangerous, so you must keep back from them.
explain: The plural, fireworks, often means a whole show of them, as in a firework display. Some words have more than one meaning, so check the clues. Informally, fireworks can also mean an angry argument or something very exciting. Made-up example: «When Dad saw the broken window, there were fireworks.» Here the clue is the angry feeling, so it means an argument.
explain: Safety words in the sentences are clues too. «Diwali is the festival of lights, celebrated with lamps, sweets and fireworks» tells you fireworks are part of a celebration. «An adult soaks one that did not go off in water» tells you that only adults should handle fireworks, and that a firework that fails to go off is still dangerous.
fact: A firework is a small device with powder inside that explodes to make bright colours, sparks and loud bangs.
fact: Fireworks, in the plural, often means a show of them, such as a firework display.
fact: Fireworks are set off at celebrations such as Bonfire Night, New Year, Diwali, Canada Day and Independence Day.
fact: Different metals make different colours in fireworks, which is why they are used to show chemical reactions in science.
fact: Fireworks are explosives: only adults should light them, you should stand far back, and you should never go back to one that did not go off.
exercise: «Diwali is the festival of lights, celebrated with lamps, sweets and fireworks.» What is a firework? | *A small device with powder inside that explodes to make bright colours, sparks and loud bangs | A lamp that glows all night | A kind of sweet eaten at festivals
exercise: Dad says, «There will be fireworks when she sees the bill.» What does fireworks mean here? | A very exciting day out | A show of bright lights in the sky | *An angry argument
exercise: «Watch fireworks from a safe distance and never pick up a used firework.» What should you do with a firework that did not go off? | *Never pick it up; an adult soaks it in water | Put it in your pocket | Pick it up and try again
test: «A firework's bang comes from powder burning so fast inside a closed case that the hot gas bursts it.» What makes the bang? | A metal ball hitting the case | Sparks falling into water | *Powder burning so fast in a closed case that hot gas bursts it
test: In «The fireworks lit up the bay all night», what does fireworks most likely mean? | *A show of bright lights in the sky | A type of cake | An angry argument
exam: What should you do with a finished sparkler? | *Put it in water | Throw it in the bin while it is still hot | Wave it around to cool it down | Keep it in your pocket for later
